  "summary": "Architectural approach that seeks to create modern architecture that responds to local context and culture.",
  "description": "Critical Regionalism mediates between universal modernism and particular local cultures, using modern technology but adapting it to local traditions, climate, and materials. This approach emerged as a critique of placeless international modernism and seeks to create architecture that is both modern and rooted in its specific location.",
  "culturalContext": "Critical Regionalism — theorized by Kenneth Frampton (1983) — asks modern architecture to answer place without kitsch: Tadao Ando's concrete engaging Japanese light and weather, Jørn Utzon's Battlemented Danish-to-Sydney crossbreeds, Geoffrey Bawa's Sri Lankan verandas. Against both universal International Style and nostalgic vernacular, it extracts local climate, craft and tectonic wisdom into contemporary form — the dominant serious position for regional architecture since.",
